From d637724bf573bb0711f367695523aafcdedb0be4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daemonhorn <dhorn2000@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 15 Aug 2026 20:56:12 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] riscv64: add support for riscv64 VM guests

Adds riscv64 as a supported guest architecture, targeting TCG software
emulation on x86_64 hosts (no KVM).

Introduces uses_virt_machine($arch) in Helpers.pm and uses it to replace
the ~10 open-coded `$arch eq 'aarch64'` checks that were really asking
"does this arch use QEMU's virt machine?". riscv64 shares aarch64's
constraints here: no legacy VGA, PCIe bus naming, USB via usb-ehci, and
no vmgenid.

Fixes a TPM device-model bug that affected aarch64 as well: virt machines
expose the sysbus TPM (tpm-tis-device), not the ISA one (tpm-tis).
Verified empirically -- `-device tpm-tis,help` reports "Device 'tpm-tis'
not found" on both aarch64 and riscv64 QEMU builds, while tpm-tis-device
succeeds.

The riscv64 CPU-model load is guarded by a -f check with a hardcoded
fallback, so hosts running a pve-qemu-kvm that does not yet ship
cpu-models-riscv64.json keep working instead of dying on every VM start
for all architectures.

Adds three cfg2cmd fixtures (default host, native riscv64 host, and
riscv64-host-with-x86_64-guest), matching aarch64's coverage; the
riscv64 default-host fixture additionally exercises the TPM and UART
serial paths.

Verified on a real Proxmox VE install: `qm create --arch riscv64` is
accepted and `qm showcmd` emits qemu-system-riscv64, -cpu max,
accel=tcg,type=virt, tpm-tis-device, virtio-gpu and bus=pcie.0. A
riscv64 guest boots OpenSBI v1.7 and EDK2 UEFI firmware to the boot
manager.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
